[test-suite name=ux ring=2
  [doc [#
Conformance fixtures for cx-x/ux — the SEMANTIC CORE of the UX projection
(#787). NB cx-x/ux is an EXPERIMENTAL x/-TIER module (D3): bundled + gated but
exempt from the frozen-std stability promise, because #787's DP1 is an explicit
go/kill. Programs name the tier via [?lib 'cx-x/ux'].

Normative source: design/787/787-phase0-spec.md, owner-RULED 2026-08-17. Each
case names the [P0-n] clause it pins.

THIS MODULE CONTAINS NO RENDERER. W1-W4 kept the HTML lowering beside the
vocabulary and htmx concepts duly climbed into it — `target=`, `swap=`, `oob=`,
`ext=`, `subscribe=`, `on=`, one of them carrying a literal CSS selector. P0-20
(R5) claims the vocabulary is renderable by a non-browser renderer; that claim
was false, and a comment cannot make it true. The renderers now live in
cx-x/ux-web and cx-x/ux-tui, and the six leaked attributes are REFUSED here.

The load-bearing properties, and why each is a fixture rather than a comment:

  IDENTITY (P0-8/P0-9). A fragment address is exactly (route, feature instance,
  element path). Theme, template, locale, actor, session and data are inputs to
  a fragment's CONTENT, never its IDENTITY.

  DERIVED IDS (P0-11, Q-B). The DOM id is `cx-` + the first 16 hex chars of the
  sha2-256 of the address's strict canonical bytes. `frag-id-of` derives the
  same id from an already-computed canonical form, byte-identically — the
  optimization is pinned so it cannot drift into a second identity.

  NEUTRALITY (P0-20). A semantic element carrying a renderer-private attribute
  is refused. This is the fixture that keeps the vocabulary honest; the module
  boundary is the structure, and this is the check.

  HONEST KEYING (P0-10). A row keys by its collection's DECLARED key. An
  unkeyed row gets NO address rather than a positional one.

  THE PATCH ALGEBRA (P0-44). Live frames lower onto insert/update/remove/
  repaint over a keyed collection addressed by ELEMENT PATH — never onto a
  renderer's swap syntax.

  THE CONTENT NORMAL FORM. What two renderings are asserted to agree on. It
  normalizes sequence nesting away, because a projected tree and its
  hand-authored twin are the same content and structure.

Every expected output below was re-derived by RUNNING the program against the
shipped binary (cx <file> — the single oracle).
#]]

 [# The address is a CX value whose STRICT CANONICAL BYTES are the identity; the
    Tier-1 address is tagged sha2-256:<hex> like every other address in the suite,
    and the DOM id is the first 16 hex chars of it. Both are stated functions of
    the same bytes — never a second identity. #]
 [case id=ux-001-fragment-address-and-dom-id level=core
  [tags ux frag identity dom-id P0-9 P0-11]
  [title 'fragment address and its derived hash16 DOM id']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let [= $f [$ux:frag "/orders/o-1041" "orders" "/open-orders/status"]]
  [list [$ux:frag-address $f] [$ux:frag-id $f]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'sha2-256:e1029934ea3a0804f9d5aab65a3933242fb692cbf15eeac4b7bb83c6899025d6' 'cx-e1029934ea3a0804']
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-8: the identity inputs are EXACTLY the triple. Same triple, same id; a
    different feature INSTANCE is a different fragment even on the same route with
    the same path — which is why composing one feature twice on a route without
    distinct instance names is a refusal. #]
 [case id=ux-002-identity-is-exactly-the-triple level=core
  [tags ux frag identity P0-8]
  [title 'equal triples address equally; a different instance does not']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let [= $a [$ux:frag "/orders" "orders" "/open"]]
      [= $b [$ux:frag "/orders" "orders" "/open"]]
      [= $c [$ux:frag "/orders" "orders-b" "/open"]]
  [list [= [$ux:frag-id $a] [$ux:frag-id $b]] [= [$ux:frag-id $a] [$ux:frag-id $c]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list true false]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-10: a declared row key yields a row step; NO key yields absence, not a
    positional index. Absence-in, absence-out. #]
 [case id=ux-003-row-and-path-steps level=core
  [tags ux path row-key keying P0-10]
  [; R-A1 migration: absence contributes nothing (2026-08-25) ]
  [title 'element-path steps, and absence for an unkeyed collection']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [$ux:row-step "/open" "o-1041"] [$ux:row-step "/open" ()] [$ux:path-step "/" "grid"] [$ux:path-step "/open" "grid"]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '/open/row[o-1041]' '/grid' '/open/grid']
#]]
 ]

 [# An addressed element needs BOTH the DOM id and the readable form, and computing
    them independently canonicalizes the same value twice — measured at 56 us vs
    30 us per element. `frag-id-of` derives the id from the repr instead. Sound
    because [$cx:hash] IS "sha2-256:" followed by the sha2-256 of exactly those
    bytes. Pinned here so an optimization can never become a second identity. #]
 [case id=ux-004-frag-id-of-is-the-same-identity level=core
  [tags ux frag identity perf P0-11]
  [title 'the id derived from a computed repr is byte-identical to the id derived from the address']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let [= $f [$ux:frag "/orders" "orders" "/open"]]
  [list [$ux:frag-id $f] [$ux:frag-id-of [$ux:frag-repr $f]]
        [= [$ux:frag-id $f] [$ux:frag-id-of [$ux:frag-repr $f]]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'cx-2905217911f2a7e8' 'cx-2905217911f2a7e8' true]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-20 rules the DISCIPLINE, not the final list — members are added by ruling,
    never by convenience. `ux:filter` is the one W5 addition (owner, 2026-08-17):
    a filter is a QUERY, with no effect, nothing committed and nothing journaled,
    so it cannot ride [ux:form] without lying about what invoking it does. #]
 [case id=ux-010-the-semantic-vocabulary level=core
  [tags ux vocabulary neutrality P0-20]
  [title 'the semantic element set, and the renderer-private attributes it refuses']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
[list [?splice [$ux:vocabulary]] [?splice [$ux:renderer-private-attrs]] [?splice [$ux:tones]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'ux:region' 'ux:card' 'ux:heading' 'ux:text' 'ux:field' 'ux:badge' 'ux:list' 'ux:item' 'ux:table' 'ux:columns' 'ux:column' 'ux:rows' 'ux:row' 'ux:cell' 'ux:nav' 'ux:nav-item' 'ux:link' 'ux:action' 'ux:form' 'ux:input' 'ux:hidden' 'ux:submit' 'ux:error' 'ux:notice' 'ux:filter' 'ux:select' 'ux:option' 'ux:group' 'ux:media' 'ux:grid' 'ux:param' 'ux:steps' 'ux:step' 'ux:quantity' 'ux:breadcrumb' 'ux:crumb' 'ux:pager' 'ux:page-link' 'ux:facet' 'ux:facet-value' 'ux:sort' 'ux:sort-option' 'ux:aside' 'ux:status' 'ux:panel' 'oob' 'target' 'swap' 'ext' 'subscribe' 'on' 'include' 'ok' 'warn' 'danger' 'info' 'neutral']
#]]
 ]

 [# THE NEUTRALITY GATE. Each of these was a real attribute on a real semantic
    element in W1-W4, and `oob=` carried a literal CSS selector — a terminal
    renderer handed such a tree would have had to parse htmx swap syntax to learn
    what the author meant. What they MEANT is now said semantically (`live=`,
    `affects=`) with the transport in the render context. #]
 [case id=ux-011-renderer-private-attrs-are-refused level=core
  [tags ux neutrality refusal P0-20 R5]
  [title 'the six htmx attributes that leaked into the vocabulary are now refusals']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $bad ([ux:region feature=o oob="innerHTML:#x"],
           [ux:region feature=o ext=sse],
           [ux:region feature=o subscribe="/stream"],
           [ux:region feature=o on=changed],
           [ux:action verb=v target="#x"],
           [ux:action verb=v swap=outerHTML],
           [ux:action verb=v include="#f"])]
  [?for [in $e $bad] [yield [$ux:refusals-of $e]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:region attr=oob]
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:region attr=ext]
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:region attr=subscribe]
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:region attr=on]
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:action attr=target]
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:action attr=swap]
[ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:action attr=include]
#]]
 ]

 [# The replacements are not spellings of the same thing: `live=` names a LOGICAL
    FEED (a name, not an endpoint) and `affects=` names an ELEMENT PATH (an
    address, not a selector). Both are resolvable by any renderer. #]
 [case id=ux-012-semantic-declarations-are-accepted level=core
  [tags ux neutrality P0-20]
  [title 'live= and affects= are semantic, and pass the gate the six do not']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let [= $t [ux:region feature=orders path="/live" live=orders
                [ux:action verb=place-order affects="/live/new" label="Go"]
                [ux:filter name=q affects="/live/open/rows"]]]
  [; asserting the ABSENCE of refusals and nothing else is a golden that stays
     green if the gate is deleted — so the surviving declarations are asserted
     too, positively. ]
  [= $act [$first $t//ux:action]]
  [= $flt [$first $t//ux:filter]]
  [list [$exists [$ux:refusals-of $t]] [$string $t@live] [$string $act@affects] [$string $flt@affects]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list false 'orders' '/live/new' '/live/open/rows']
#]]
 ]

 [# Every refusal is collected BEFORE lowering, so the lowering is total and an
    [err] can never end up spliced into a rendered page as if it were content. #]
 [case id=ux-013-semantic-tree-refusals level=core
  [tags ux refusal validation]
  [title 'the per-member payload rules, collected before any lowering']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $bad ([ux:nope], [ux:heading level=9 "x"], [ux:badge tone=purple "x"],
           [ux:action label=x], [ux:form], [ux:input], [ux:region],
           [ux:link id=nope href="/x"], [ux:filter name=q])]
  [?for [in $e $bad] [yield [$ux:refusals-of $e]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux-refusal code=ux-unknown-element element=ux:nope]
[ux-refusal code=ux-bad-heading-level element=ux:heading level='9']
[ux-refusal code=ux-bad-tone element=ux:badge tone=purple]
[ux-refusal code=ux-action-no-verb element=ux:action]
[err code=cx-err:CXER0100 message='cx-err:CXER0100: missing required argument source']
[ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:input]
[ux-refusal code=ux-region-no-feature element=ux:region]
[ux-refusal code=ux-id-not-addressable element=ux:link id=nope]
[ux-refusal code=ux-unknown-attr element=ux:link attr=id]
[ux-refusal code=ux-filter-no-affects element=ux:filter]
#]]
 ]

 [# Mixed content is refused so the lowering can assume exactly one of the two
    shapes applies to any given element — which is what lets `content-leaf` be
    total in all three modules. #]
 [case id=ux-014-mixed-content-is-refused level=core
  [tags ux refusal validation]
  [title 'an element carrying both element children and text is refused']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let [= $m [ux:card [ux:text "a"] "loose text"]] [$ux:refusals-of $m]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux-refusal code=ux-mixed-content element=ux:card]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-98 (RULED 2026-08-18, audit letter 3a). The demonstration bug: [ux:action
    disabled=true reason=...] passed the gate and both renderers ignored it —
    the same silent-acceptance family as the campaign's three language traps.
    Also pinned: the store's own aside name= (read by nobody, the first thing
    the closure caught), a row id= (a row addresses by key=, so an id on it was
    silently discarded), and the dead W1-era filter over=. #]
 [case id=ux-015-unknown-attributes-are-refused level=core
  [tags ux refusal attribute-closure P0-98]
  [title 'an attribute no clause grants to its member is a refusal, not a silence']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $bad ([ux:action verb=v label=x disabled=true reason="out"],
           [ux:aside label=Basket name=basket],
           [ux:row id=r1 [ux:cell "x"]],
           [ux:filter name=q affects="/rows" over="id status"])]
  [?for [in $e $bad] [yield [$ux:refusals-of $e]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux-refusal code=ux-unknown-attr element=ux:action attr=disabled]
[ux-refusal code=ux-unknown-attr element=ux:aside attr=name]
[ux-refusal code=ux-unknown-attr element=ux:row attr=id]
[ux-refusal code=ux-unknown-attr element=ux:filter attr=over]
#]]
 ]

 [# The closure's other half: everything a clause grants or the shared layer
    honors stays admitted — including the projection-emitted attributes a
    table derived from the content normal form alone would have refused
    (prominence on an action, repeat/help/add-label on a group, value/step on
    a quantity). The action and aside rows of the table are pinned explicitly:
    an attribute added to a member without a ruling should move this golden. #]
 [case id=ux-016-the-attribute-grant-table level=core
  [tags ux vocabulary attribute-closure P0-98]
  [title 'granted attributes pass, and the per-member grant table is pinned']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $t [ux:region feature=browse path="/"
          [ux:card id=hero
            [ux:heading level=2 "Pans"]
            [ux:field label=Price format=money currency=USD "1200.50"]
            [ux:grid density=comfortable
              [ux:item [ux:media src="/a.svg" alt="a pan" kind=illustration tone=neutral]]]
            [ux:action verb=add-to-basket label="Add" prominence=primary affects="/hero"
              [ux:param name=sku value=FK-0119]]
            [ux:group name=lines label=Lines repeat=true add-label="Add a line"
              [ux:input name=qty kind=int help="How many"]]
            [ux:quantity name=qty label=Quantity verb=set-quantity value=2 min=1 max=9 step=1]
            [ux:aside label="Your basket" open=true dismiss-href="/x"]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [$exists [$ux:refusals-of $t]]
        [?splice [$ux:allowed-attrs "ux:action"]] [?splice [$ux:allowed-attrs "ux:aside"]] [?splice [$ux:allowed-attrs "ux:row"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list false 'verb' 'label' 'affects' 'confirm' 'confirm-href' 'prominence' 'available' 'reason' 'label' 'open' 'dismiss-href' 'id' 'live' 'key']
#]]
 ]

 [# The seven renderer-private names are recognized mistakes with their own
    refusal code naming the kind of mistake; the closure must not ALSO refuse
    them as unknown. One mistake, one refusal, the more precise name. #]
 [case id=ux-017-one-mistake-one-refusal level=core
  [tags ux refusal attribute-closure P0-98 P0-20]
  [title 'a renderer-private attribute refuses ONCE, with its own code, never twice']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $r [$ux:refusals-of [ux:action verb=v target="#x"]]]
  [= $f [$first $r]]
  [list [$count $r] [$string $f@code]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 1 'ux-renderer-private-attr']
#]]
 ]

 [# Found by the P0-98 attribute inventory: three members carry a tone and only
    two checked it, so [ux:notice tone=sparkly] passed. Same closed set (2.5),
    same refusal code. #]
 [case id=ux-018-notice-tone-joins-the-closed-set level=core
  [tags ux refusal validation tone]
  [title 'ux:notice validates its tone against the closed set, as badge and status always did']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:notice tone=sparkly "hi"]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:notice tone=ok "saved"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-tone element=ux:notice tone=sparkly]]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-99 (RULED 2026-08-18, audit letter 2a). A disabled control with no stated
    why is a locked door with no sign on it: available=false REQUIRES reason=,
    available= is boolean-shaped and closed (the tone precedent), and the normal
    form carries both verbatim — an inert act whose why one face drops is two
    stores telling different truths. confirm= became presence-boolean in the
    same entry (the audit's H5 blemish: confirm='' made 'no confirm' and 'empty
    confirm' indistinguishable), and a confirming act carries NO verb: its web
    rendering is a link to the confirmation view and the verb lives there. #]
 [case id=ux-019-an-unavailable-act-says-so-and-says-why level=core
  [tags ux refusal availability P0-99 P0-47]
  [title 'available=false requires reason=; both are content; capability absence stays P0-47']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $noreason [$ux:refusals-of [ux:action verb=buy label=B available=false]]]
  [= $badval   [$ux:refusals-of [ux:action verb=buy label=B available=maybe reason=x]]]
  [= $ok       [$ux:refusals-of [ux:action verb=buy label=B available=false reason="Out of stock"]]]
  [list [?splice $noreason] [?splice $badval] [$exists $ok]
        [$ux:content-of [ux:action verb=buy label=B available=false reason="Out of stock"
                          [ux:param name=sku value=X-1]]]
        [$ux:content-of [ux:action verb=buy label=B]]
        [$ux:content-of [ux:action verb=empty label=E confirm="Removes everything." confirm-href="/x/empty"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-unavailable-no-reason element=ux:action verb=buy] [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-available element=ux:action available=maybe] false [c:action verb=buy label=B available='false' reason='Out of stock' [c:param name=sku value=X-1]] [c:action verb=buy label=B] [c:action label='E' confirm='true']]
#]]
 ]

 [# Audit H6 / register L5: equivalence proves agreement, not completeness — the
    product page had no buy control for three waves and every fixture certified
    both faces rendered the absence identically. act-verbs takes the feature's
    effect=act verbs (the P0-17 tools-projection discriminator); control-verbs
    takes the deduped verbs a tree offers as controls (actions — available or
    not — forms, quantitys); verbs-uncovered diffs a route's declared offers
    against them; verbs-unaccounted holds the whole surface to the feature. #]
 [case id=ux-022-verb-coverage-is-mechanical level=core
  [tags ux coverage completeness H6 P0-17 P0-86 W16]
  [title 'invokable verbs diff against emitted controls; absence refuses unless declared']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $f [feature name=shop
          [verbs
            [verb name=buy effect=act [summary "x"]]
            [verb name=cancel effect=act [summary "y"]]
            [verb name=return effect=act [summary "z"]]
            [verb name=browse effect=observe]]]]
  [= $t [ux:region feature=shop path="/"
          [ux:action verb=buy label=B [ux:param name=sku value=X]]
          [ux:action verb=buy label=B2 available=false reason="Out of stock"
            [ux:param name=sku value=Y]]
          [ux:form verb=cancel [ux:input name=id] [ux:submit "Go"]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:act-verbs $f]] [?splice [$ux:control-verbs $t]]
        [?splice [$ux:verbs-uncovered ("buy", "cancel") $t]]
        [?splice [$ux:verbs-uncovered ("buy", "return") $t]]
        [?splice [$ux:verbs-unaccounted $f ("buy", "cancel") ("return")]]
        [?splice [$ux:verbs-unaccounted $f ("buy") ()]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'buy' 'cancel' 'return' 'buy' 'cancel' [ux-refusal code=ux-verb-uncovered verb=return] [ux-refusal code=ux-verb-unaccounted verb=cancel] [ux-refusal code=ux-verb-unaccounted verb=return]]
#]]
 ]

 [# A region that renders correctly once and then silently never updates is the
    precise failure class 5.4 exists to exclude — it passes every server-side
    gate. Checked in the CORE, over the tree and its context, because it is a
    property of neither HTML nor a terminal. #]
 [case id=ux-020-an-unbound-feed-is-refused level=core
  [tags ux liveness binding refusal P0-44]
  [title 'a region naming a feed the render context does not bind refuses, on every renderer']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx [feed name=orders endpoint="/stream" event="orders-changed"]]]
  [= $ok  [ux:region feature=orders path="/live" live=orders]]
  [= $bad [ux:region feature=orders path="/live" live=invoices]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:binding-refusals $ok $rctx]] [?splice [$ux:binding-refusals $bad $rctx]]
        [?splice [$ux:feed-binding $rctx "orders"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-unbound-feed feed=invoices] [feed name=orders endpoint='/stream' event=orders-changed]]
#]]
 ]

 [# The ONE genuinely web-shaped entry in the render context, and deliberately
    left as such: the terminal renderer never reads it. A renderer-specific hint
    another renderer IGNORES is the right shape; one another renderer must PARSE
    is what the neutrality work removed from the vocabulary. #]
 [case id=ux-021-swap-mode-resolution level=core
  [tags ux render-context web-hint]
  [title 'swap modes resolve per affects path, then the default, then outerHTML']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $r [ux:render-ctx [swap default=morph] [swap affects="/a/b" mode=innerHTML]]]
  [= $e [ux:render-ctx]]
  [list [$ux:swap-mode $r "/a/b"] [$ux:swap-mode $r "/other"] [$ux:swap-mode $e "/x"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'innerHTML' 'morph' 'outerHTML']
#]]
 ]

 [# The subject of the equivalence claim. Each renderer supplies a readback from
    the tree it actually emits to THIS form, and the three canonical bytes are
    compared. `kind=` is deliberately absent: the tree carries a CX TYPE and each
    renderer maps it to its own control many-to-one, so asserting on it would
    assert that two renderers make the same widget choice. #]
 [case id=ux-030-content-normal-form level=core
  [tags ux equivalence normal-form R5]
  [title 'the common content normal form: content and structure, presentation stripped']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let [= $t [ux:card
                [ux:heading level=2 "Open orders"]
                [ux:field label="Customer" "Ada"]
                [ux:field label="Status" [ux:badge tone=warn "late"]]
                [ux:input name=qty kind=int required=true]
                [ux:action verb=place-order label="Place order"]]]
  [$ux:content-repr $t]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
"[c:card\n  [c:heading level='2' Open orders]\n  [c:field label=Customer Ada]\n  [c:field label=Status\n    [c:badge tone=warn late]\n  ]\n  [c:input name=qty label=Qty required='true' error='' '']\n  [c:action verb=place-order label='Place order']\n]\n"
#]]
 ]

 [# A hand-authored list has element children; the SAME list built by a projection
    has one sequence-valued child holding them. Both are the same content and the
    same structure, and a normal form that distinguished them would report every
    projected tree as unequal to its hand-authored twin — which is the comparison
    W5 exists to make. #]
 [case id=ux-031-sequence-nesting-is-normalized-away level=core
  [tags ux equivalence normal-form]
  [title 'a projected collection and its hand-authored twin have the same normal form']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $hand [ux:list [ux:item "a"] [ux:item "b"]]]
  [= $proj [ux:list [?for [in $x ("a","b")] [yield [ux:item $x]]]]]
  [list [= [$ux:content-repr $hand] [$ux:content-repr $proj]] [$ux:content-repr $proj]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list true '[c:list\n  [c:item a]\n  [c:item b]\n]\n']
#]]
 ]

 [# Which elements own an address is a property of the SEMANTIC TREE, not of a
    renderer, so it is stated once and both renderers mint from it. Until W5 this
    lived in the web emitter over the lowered HTML tree — which left the terminal
    renderer with no duplicate check at all while minting its focus ring from the
    same ids. #]
 [case id=ux-040-minted-addresses level=core
  [tags ux addressing P0-11 P0-16]
  [title 'every (DOM id, readable address) a semantic tree will mint, from the semantic tree']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $t [ux:region feature=orders path="/live"
          [ux:card id=open [ux:heading level=2 "Open"]]
          [ux:form verb=place-order [ux:submit label="Go"]]]]
  [?for [in $p [$ux:address-pairs $t {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/"}]]
    [yield [$string $p@id]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'cx-b9aa0e4902fe6a43'
'cx-70c02252bf05598f'
'cx-25ff65bbcc985af2'
#]]
 ]

 [# TWO causes, reported distinctly because they send an implementer to different
    places: ux-id-collision is the hash16 TRUNCATION collision P0-11 names, and
    ux-address-reused is the projection producing two fragments that claim to be
    the same fragment — an element path that failed to distinguish them. W3 found
    the second for real. #]
 [case id=ux-041-a-reused-address-is-refused level=core
  [tags ux addressing refusal P0-11]
  [title 'two placed elements minting the same address refuse; distinct ones do not']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $ctx {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/"}]
  [= $dup [ux:region feature=f path="/r" [ux:card id=same [ux:text "a"]] [ux:card id=same [ux:text "b"]]]]
  [= $ok  [ux:region feature=f path="/r" [ux:card id=one [ux:text "a"]] [ux:card id=two [ux:text "b"]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:address-collisions $dup $ctx]] [?splice [$ux:address-collisions $ok $ctx]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-address-reused id=cx-bfb773fba6d60c77]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Columns derive from the ROW SCHEMA, so a table needs no column list and nothing
    that could drift from the data. The row container carries an id so a live
    patch has a real address to append into — W4b's feed fell back to wiping the
    whole region for want of one. #]
 [case id=ux-050-table-projection level=core
  [tags ux table projection keying P0-10 P0-17]
  [title 'columns derive from the row schema; the row container is addressed']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rows
     ([order id=o-1041 customer="Ada Lovelace" total=42.50 status=late
        [line-items [line sku=ESC-18 qty=2 price=15.00] [line sku=MSP-4 qty=1 price=12.50]]],
      [order id=o-1042 customer="Lin Wu" total=7.00 status=on-time
        [line-items [line sku=BAL-9 qty=1 price=7.00]]])]
  [= $types ([row-type name=order key=id], [row-type name=line key=sku])]
  [$ux:table $rows {id: "open", types: $types}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux:table id=open [ux:columns [ux:column 'Customer'] [ux:column 'Id'] [ux:column 'Status'] [ux:column 'Total'] [ux:column 'Line items']] [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=o-1041 [ux:cell 'Ada Lovelace'] [ux:cell 'o-1041'] [ux:cell 'late'] [ux:cell '42.50'] [ux:cell [ux:table id=line-items [ux:columns [ux:column 'Price'] [ux:column 'Qty'] [ux:column 'Sku']] [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=ESC-18 [ux:cell '15.00'] [ux:cell '2'] [ux:cell 'ESC-18']] [ux:row key=MSP-4 [ux:cell '12.50'] [ux:cell '1'] [ux:cell 'MSP-4']]]]]] [ux:row key=o-1042 [ux:cell 'Lin Wu'] [ux:cell 'o-1042'] [ux:cell 'on-time'] [ux:cell '7.00'] [ux:cell [ux:table id=line-items [ux:columns [ux:column 'Price'] [ux:column 'Qty'] [ux:column 'Sku']] [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=BAL-9 [ux:cell '7.00'] [ux:cell '1'] [ux:cell 'BAL-9']]]]]]]]
#]]
 ]

 [# An `id` attribute is not treated as identity because it is spelled `id`: that
    would be a naming convention masquerading as a keying rule. Declaring the key
    is what earns per-row addresses; absence is answered with absence. #]
 [case id=ux-051-unkeyed-rows-get-no-address level=core
  [tags ux keying honesty P0-10]
  [title 'a collection with no declared key gets no per-row addresses, honestly']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rows ([order id=o-1 customer="Ada"], [order id=o-2 customer="Lin"])]
  [$ux:table $rows {id: "open"}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux:table id=open [ux:columns [ux:column 'Customer'] [ux:column 'Id']] [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell 'o-1']] [ux:row [ux:cell 'Lin'] [ux:cell 'o-2']]]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Drill-down is NAVIGATION, which the vocabulary already had — so it needed no
    new member, unlike filtering, which is a query. The web renderer makes the
    link an anchor; the terminal renderer activates it from the focused row. Only
    the key column, and only when a key is declared: a row with no identity has
    nothing to drill into. #]
 [case id=ux-052-drill-down-needs-no-new-vocabulary level=core
  [tags ux drill-down navigation W5]
  [title 'a detail route puts an ordinary link in the key column, and only there']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rows ([order id=o-1 customer="Ada"])]
  [= $types ([row-type name=order key=id])]
  [list [$ux:table $rows {id: "open", types: $types, detail-route: "/orders"}]
        [$ux:table $rows {id: "open", detail-route: "/orders"}]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux:table id=open [ux:columns [ux:column 'Customer'] [ux:column 'Id']] [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=o-1 [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell [ux:link href='/orders/o-1' 'o-1']]]]] [ux:table id=open [ux:columns [ux:column 'Customer'] [ux:column 'Id']] [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell 'o-1']]]]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Clause PRESENCE is the whole discriminator (stream 6), zero-item included —
    never an item count, never a name convention. Required is the non-defaulted
    POSITIONALS, exactly the rule the tools projection uses, so the two faces of
    one command agree on what a caller must supply. Labels degrade to
    name-derived; the tools face fails loud there and this one does not, because
    human-facing defaults are presentational. #]
 [case id=ux-060-form-projection-from-a-command-def level=core
  [tags ux form projection P0-17 P0-18]
  [; R-A1 migration: absence contributes nothing (2026-08-25) ]
  [title 'a [?def] with an [effects] clause projects onto a semantic form']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $src """
[?def place-order scope=public impure [effects [write]] [returns element]
  ($customer::string $qty::int $note::string="")
  [ordered customer=$customer]]
[fn-doc name=place-order scope=public purity=impure
  [summary '''Place a customer order.''']
  [param-doc name=customer '''Who the order is for.''']]
"""]
  [$ux:form $src "place-order" {affects: "/live/new"}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux:form verb=place-order affects='/live/new' [ux:heading level=2 'Place a customer order.'] [ux:input name=customer kind=string required='true' help='Who the order is for.'] [ux:input name=qty kind=int required='true'] [ux:input name=note kind=string required='false'] [ux:submit label='Place order']]
#]]
 ]

 [# The command layer enforces the rule once; the projection renders whatever it
    returned. The error CODE is deliberately not rendered — the message is what
    the command layer chose to disclose, and a code is structure the refused
    actor has no claim on. #]
 [case id=ux-061-a-refusal-carries-its-own-words level=core
  [tags ux form-error P0-17]
  [title 'the UX layer never restates a rule']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[$ux:form-error "a customer and a quantity of at least 1 are required"]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux:error [ux:text 'a customer and a quantity of at least 1 are required']]
#]]
 ]

 [# A form for a command that does not exist would post to an intent wire that
    cannot dispatch it. #]
 [case id=ux-062-no-such-command-refuses level=core
  [tags ux form refusal]
  [title 'projecting a verb the source does not define is a refusal']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
[$ux:form "[?def other pure [returns int] () 1]" "place-order" {}]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-no-such-command verb=place-order]]
#]]
 ]

 [# A NAMED COLUMN IS NEVER DROPPED. Columns the claim omits keep their derived
    order and follow the named ones, so a stale claim — one written against an
    older schema that has since gained a field — degrades to "the fields I named
    first, then the rest" rather than silently hiding data. A presentation claim
    must never be able to make a value disappear. #]
 [case id=ux-070-order-hint-never-drops-a-column level=core
  [tags ux hints claims P0-21 P0-7]
  [title 'a partial order claim reorders what it names and keeps the rest']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rows
     ([order id=o-1041 customer="Ada Lovelace" total=42.50 status=late
        [line-items [line sku=ESC-18 qty=2 price=15.00] [line sku=MSP-4 qty=1 price=12.50]]],
      [order id=o-1042 customer="Lin Wu" total=7.00 status=on-time
        [line-items [line sku=BAL-9 qty=1 price=7.00]]])]
  [= $types ([row-type name=order key=id], [row-type name=line key=sku])]
  [= $addr [$ux:schema-address [$cx:parse "[schema [order [attr id::text] [attr customer::text]]]"]]]
  [= $hints ([ux:hint order="status id" [schema $addr] [path "/"]])]
  [= $t [$ux:table $rows {id: "open", types: $types, hints: $hints}]]
  [?for [in $c $t//ux:column] [yield [$string $c]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'Customer'
'Id'
'Status'
'Total'
'Line items'
'Price'
'Qty'
'Sku'
'Price'
'Qty'
'Sku'
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-4's composite string form is the tagged address immediately followed by the
    CXPath — unambiguous by construction, because the address grammar admits no
    `/` and every CXPath starts with one. A DERIVED SPELLING of the pair, never a
    third identity. Absence is a normal answer: cosmetics fail open (P0-7). #]
 [case id=ux-071-hint-resolution-and-the-claim-key level=core
  [tags ux hints claims P0-4 P0-6]
  [; R-A1 migration: absence contributes nothing (2026-08-25) ]
  [title 'hints resolve by (schema address, CXPath); the composite key is a derived spelling']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $hints ([ux:hint view=cards [schema "sha2-256:abc"] [path "/items"]])]
  [list [$ux:claim-key "sha2-256:abc" "/items"]
        [$ux:claim-key "sha2-256:abc" "/"]
        [$ux:hint-for $hints "sha2-256:abc" "/items" "view"]
        [$ux:hint-for $hints "sha2-256:abc" "/items" "order"]
        [$ux:hint-for $hints "sha2-256:zzz" "/items" "view"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'sha2-256:abc/items' 'sha2-256:abc/' 'cards']
#]]
 ]

 [# RULED 1a. Deriving the address from a sample was tried and is wrong:
    [$cx:schema-of] bakes INFERRED cardinality into the schema text, so an order
    with two line items and an order with one produce different addresses for the
    same shape — and a claim written against one silently stops applying to the
    other. Silently, because cosmetic claims fail open, which is the worst
    combination. #]
 [case id=ux-072-claim-subjects-are-declared-schemas level=core
  [tags ux hints claims identity RULED-1a]
  [title 'a schema address is taken from a SCHEMA, never inferred from a sample value']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $one [order id=o-1 [line-items [line sku=A]]]]
  [= $two [order id=o-2 [line-items [line sku=A] [line sku=B]]]]
  [= $inferred-1 [$ux:schema-address [$cx:parse [$cx:schema-of $one]]]]
  [= $inferred-2 [$ux:schema-address [$cx:parse [$cx:schema-of $two]]]]
  [list [= $inferred-1 $inferred-2]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list false]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-17 rules the subject as a [?def] with an [effects] clause. The in-family
    reference estate does not define commands that way — its domain is XAP FEATURE
    DOCUMENTS — so W5 could not project the reference app until this was ruled.
    The premise is one definition, three faces; if the estate's definitions are
    feature documents, the third face projects THOSE. Fields come from the noun
    the verb [writes]; the [summary] titles it, degrading exactly as the [?def]
    lane degrades.

    WHAT THIS GRAMMAR DOES NOT CARRY, and is therefore not invented here:
    optionality. A [field] declares a name, a type and sometimes a doc — there is
    no required/optional marker, so nothing is marked required. #]
 [case id=ux-080-feature-form-projects-the-xap-grammar level=core
  [tags ux feature-grammar projection RULED W5 P0-17]
  [title 'an ACT verb of a feature document projects onto a semantic form']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $f [feature name=commerce
             [nouns
               [noun name=order [field name=id type=text] [field name=package type=text]
                                [field name=buyer type=did doc="who is buying"]]
               [noun name=price [field name=amount type=money]]]
             [verbs
               [verb name=place-order effect=act [summary "Place an order"] [writes order]]
               [verb name=quote effect=observe [reads price]]]]]
  [$ux:feature-form $f "place-order" {affects: "/live/new"}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux:form verb=place-order affects='/live/new' [ux:heading level=2 'Place an order'] [ux:input name=id label=Id kind=text value=''] [ux:input name=package label=Package kind=text value=''] [ux:input name=buyer label=Buyer kind=did value='' help='who is buying'] [ux:submit label='Place order']]
#]]
 ]

 [# The declaration order is the domain author's order, so unlike the
    inferred-schema lane there is nothing alphabetical to correct with an `order`
    hint. FLAT: an earlier form nested one level, because a [?for] in an element
    body yields one sequence-valued child. A caller reading columns should not
    have to know that, so the carrier flattens it here.

    It reads its fields through `child-elements` rather than `$n/field`, because
    a named child step ATOMIZES on a single match — a noun with exactly one field
    would otherwise hand back the field's TEXT instead of the element, and the
    reference estate has such nouns. #]
 [case id=ux-081-feature-columns-is-flat level=core
  [tags ux feature-grammar projection W5]
  [title 'a noun’s fields come back as a flat column list, in the author’s declaration order']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $f [feature name=commerce
             [nouns
               [noun name=order [field name=id type=text] [field name=package type=text]
                                [field name=buyer type=did doc="who is buying"]]
               [noun name=price [field name=amount type=money]]]
             [verbs
               [verb name=place-order effect=act [summary "Place an order"] [writes order]]
               [verb name=quote effect=observe [reads price]]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:feature-columns $f "order"]]
        [?splice [$ux:feature-columns $f "price"]]
        [?splice [$ux:feature-columns $f "nonesuch"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'id' 'package' 'buyer' 'amount']
#]]
 ]

 [# Same posture as the [?def] lane: a form for a verb that does not exist would
    post to an intent wire that cannot dispatch it. #]
 [case id=ux-082-an-unknown-verb-refuses level=core
  [tags ux feature-grammar refusal W5]
  [title 'projecting a verb the feature does not declare is a refusal']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $f [feature name=commerce
             [nouns
               [noun name=order [field name=id type=text] [field name=package type=text]
                                [field name=buyer type=did doc="who is buying"]]
               [noun name=price [field name=amount type=money]]]
             [verbs
               [verb name=place-order effect=act [summary "Place an order"] [writes order]]
               [verb name=quote effect=observe [reads price]]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [$ux:feature-form $f "cancel-order" {}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-no-such-verb verb=cancel-order]]
#]]
 ]

 [# W4b lowered these straight onto htmx `hx-swap-oob` values — `beforeend:#id`,
    `delete`, `innerHTML:#id` — which put swap syntax and CSS selectors inside the
    value a feed publishes. A terminal renderer would have had to parse it. They
    now lower onto a SEMANTIC PATCH ALGEBRA addressed by ELEMENT PATH, and each
    renderer lowers that for itself.

    The container is a PATH, not a DOM id, so a renderer derives the id exactly as
    it derived the one it first rendered and the two cannot drift. #]
 [case id=ux-090-delta-frames-lower-to-a-patch-algebra level=core
  [tags ux liveness delta patch P0-44 P0-10]
  [title 'insert, regroup, retract and recompute become semantic operations over a keyed collection']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rows ([order id=o-1 customer="Ada"], [order id=o-2 customer="Lin"])]
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/open"}]
  [= $opts {types: ([row-type name=order key=id]), container: "/open/rows"}]
  [= $frames ([insert pos=2 [order id=o-3 customer="Wu"]], [regroup pos=0 [order id=o-1 customer="Ada"]], [retract pos=0], [recompute reason=refresh])]
  [ops [?splice [$ux:feed-ops $frames $rows $ctx $opts]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ops [ux:op kind=insert container='/open/rows' [ux:row key=o-3 [ux:cell 'Wu'] [ux:cell 'o-3']]] [ux:op kind=update container='/open/rows' [ux:row key=o-1 [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell 'o-1']]] [ux:op kind=remove container='/open/rows' key=o-1] [ux:op kind=repaint container='/open/rows' [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=o-1 [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell 'o-1']] [ux:row key=o-2 [ux:cell 'Lin'] [ux:cell 'o-2']]]]]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-10's honest consequence at the liveness layer: with no declared key there
    are no row addresses, so there is nothing for a targeted operation to name.
    Not a limitation being routed around — minting a positional address to make
    per-row updates possible would break on the first insertion.

    The repaint CARRIES THE ROWS. W4b's emitted an empty region with an innerHTML
    swap, which would have wiped the region it meant to refresh; the demo hid it
    by publishing a separately-rendered region and dropping the op on the floor.
    An operation nobody could consume was the tell. #]
 [case id=ux-091-unkeyed-feeds-collapse-to-repaint level=core
  [tags ux liveness row-key honesty P0-44 P0-10]
  [title 'without a declared key every frame becomes a repaint carrying the rows']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $rows ([order id=o-1 customer="Ada"])]
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/open"}]
  [= $frames ([insert pos=1 [order id=o-2 customer="Lin"]], [retract pos=0])]
  [ops [?splice [$ux:feed-ops $frames $rows $ctx {container: "/open/rows"}]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ops [ux:op kind=repaint container='/open/rows' [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell 'o-1']]]] [ux:op kind=repaint container='/open/rows' [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row [ux:cell 'Ada'] [ux:cell 'o-1']]]]]
#]]
 ]

 [# One declaration reaches a browser (one SSE connection per page, never polling)
    and a terminal (one held-open connection, likewise never polling) without
    either transport being spelled in the tree. #]
 [case id=ux-092-a-feed-names-a-logical-feed level=core
  [tags ux liveness P0-44 R5]
  [title 'the live region names a feed, not an endpoint']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[$ux:feed "orders" "/open-orders/live" "orders" [ux:text "live"]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[ux:region feature=orders path='/open-orders/live' live=orders [ux:text 'live']]
#]]
 ]

 [# A filter narrows a collection by re-fetching just that collection, so a surface
    has endpoints that serve a REGION instead of a route. Declaring them under
    their route keeps them inside the correspondence check rather than exempt from
    it — an unrouted fragment endpoint is as much a page outside the composition
    as an unrouted page is. #]
 [case id=ux-100-surface-routes-and-fragment-endpoints level=core
  [tags ux surface routing P0-24 P0-25]
  [title 'a surface document’s route table, and the fragment endpoints declared under it']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $s [surface admin
          [route path="/products" [feature xap=catalog as=catalog]]
          [route path="/orders" [feature xap=orders as=orders] [fragment path="/orders/rows"]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:routes-of $s]] [?splice [$ux:fragment-paths $s]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux:route [path '/products'] [feature 'catalog'] [instance 'catalog'] [template 'default']] [ux:route [path '/orders'] [feature 'orders'] [instance 'orders'] [template 'default']] '/orders/rows']
#]]
 ]

 [# No silent ordinals, no first-wins: the instance name is an identity input to
    every fragment address on that route. #]
 [case id=ux-101-duplicate-instances-refuse level=core
  [tags ux surface refusal P0-9]
  [title 'composing one feature twice on a route without distinct instance names is a compose-time refusal']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $dup [surface a [route path="/o" [feature xap=orders]] [route path="/o" [feature xap=orders]]]]
  [= $ok  [surface a [route path="/o" [feature xap=orders]] [route path="/o" [feature xap=orders as=second]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:duplicate-instances $dup]] [?splice [$ux:duplicate-instances $ok]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-duplicate-instance path='/o' instance=orders]]
#]]
 ]

 [# [?http-service] collects its resources as static program literals, so a route
    table cannot be computed into it — which is exactly why the correspondence is
    CHECKED rather than generated. There is no second router. The third argument
    is the composed feature documents (W16-b); a surface composing none passes
    the empty sequence and asserts nothing about verbs. #]
 [case id=ux-102-route-resource-drift-both-ways level=core
  [tags ux surface routing P0-25]
  [title 'a route with no resource and a resource with no route are both refusals']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $s [surface a [route path="/o" [feature xap=orders] [fragment path="/o/rows"]]]]
  ([$ux:check-surface $s ("/o", "/o/rows") ()],
   [$ux:check-surface $s ("/o") ()],
   [$ux:check-surface $s ("/o", "/o/rows", "/stray") ()])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
(true, [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-route-unserved path='/o/rows']], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-resource-unrouted path='/stray']])
#]]
 ]

 [# The static half of verb coverage, priced at compose time (DP4 letter e).
    Three polarities in one case: a fully accounted surface composes; a surface
    missing a verb refuses with the RULED code (ux-verb-unaccounted); and a
    [not-offered] with no why= does not account for its verb — a reasoned
    absence is a state the surface can be held to, an unreasoned one is the
    hole with a label on it, so the refusal keeps the ruled code and names the
    sharper cause in a note. #]
 [case id=ux-103-an-unaccounted-verb-refuses-at-compose-time level=core
  [tags ux surface refusal verb-coverage W16]
  [title 'an act verb no route offers and no reasoned not-offered accounts for refuses the composition']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $f [feature name=shop version="1"
          [nouns [noun name=order [field name=id type=text]]]
          [verbs
           [verb name=sell effect=act [intent [do :sell [id]]] [writes order]]
           [verb name=refund effect=act [intent [do :refund [id]]] [writes order]]
           [verb name=audit effect=act [intent [do :audit [id]]] [writes order]]
           [verb name=browse effect=observe [intent [do :browse]] [reads order]]]]]
  [= $ok  [surface a [route path="/o" [feature xap=shop] [offer verb=sell]]
            [not-offered verb=refund why="No payments rail on this surface."]
            [not-offered verb=audit why="The audit trail is the journal itself."]]]
  [= $missing [surface a [route path="/o" [feature xap=shop] [offer verb=sell]]
                [not-offered verb=refund why="No payments rail on this surface."]]]
  [= $bare [surface a [route path="/o" [feature xap=shop] [offer verb=sell]]
             [not-offered verb=refund why="No payments rail on this surface."]
             [not-offered verb=audit]]]
  ([$ux:check-surface $ok ("/o") ($f)],
   [$ux:check-surface $missing ("/o") ($f)],
   [$ux:check-surface $bare ("/o") ($f)])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
(true, [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-verb-unaccounted verb=audit]], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-verb-unaccounted verb=audit note='declared not-offered without a why']])
#]]
 ]

 [# DP4 letter (c): eleven-plus members minted id= addresses the web face never
    painted — the address existed, participated in collision checks, and reached
    no swap, no anchor and no reader. The invariant is minted ⊆ painted, held
    here over the recoverable half (data-cx-frag): structural members paint id
    AND data-cx-frag (box-x's pattern); form controls paint data-cx-frag only,
    because their DOM id is the label-binding id and an id is a derived
    rendering of the address, never a second identity. #]
 [case id=ux-104-minted-addresses-are-painted level=core
  [tags ux addressing web DP4-c]
  [title 'every address the walk mints for a placed element reaches the web DOM']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings' :as strings]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $t [ux:region feature=f path="/probe"
          [ux:heading id=h1 level=1 "Probe"]
          [ux:text id=t1 "prose"]
          [ux:card id=c1
            [ux:media id=m1 src="/x.svg" alt="a thing" kind=illustration]
            [ux:field id=f1 label="Price" "9.00"]
            [ux:badge id=b1 tone=ok "Fine"]]
          [ux:panel name=narrow
            [ux:facet id=fc1 name=material label="Material"
              [ux:facet-value id=fv1 value=iron label="Iron" count="3" href="/x?f=iron"]]]
          [ux:sort id=so1 name=sort label="Sort by"
            [ux:sort-option id=sp1 value=price label="Price" href="/x?s=price" selected=false]]
          [ux:list id=ls1 [ux:item id=it1 "one"]]
          [ux:table id=tb1 [ux:column id=co1 "Name"] [ux:row key=r1 [ux:cell id=ce1 "v"]]]
          [ux:form verb=save
            [ux:input id=in1 name=q label="Q"]
            [ux:hidden id=hd1 name=h value=v]
            [ux:select id=se1 name=s label="S" [ux:option id=op1 value=a label="A"]]
            [ux:submit id=su1 label="Save"]]
          [ux:quantity id=q1 name=qty label="Quantity" verb=set-qty value="1" min="1" max="9"]
          [ux:steps id=st1 name=flow current=one
            [ux:step id=sp2 name=one label="One" state=current]]
          [ux:breadcrumb id=bc1 [ux:crumb id=cr1 label="Home" href="/"]]
          [ux:pager id=pg1 ordered=true page="1" pages="2" total="30" prev-href="" next-href="/x?p=2"
            [ux:page-link id=pl1 n="1" href="/x?p=1" current=true]]
          [ux:status id=ss1 tone=ok "done"]
          [ux:notice id=no1 tone=info "note"]
          [ux:aside id=as1 label="Aside" open=false dismiss-href="/x"]]]
  [= $ctx {route: "/probe", feature: "f", path: "/"}]
  [= $minted [?for [in $p [$ux:address-pairs $t $ctx]] [yield [$string $p@frag]]]]
  [= $html [$web:render $t $ctx]]
  [= $painted [?for [in $seg [$strings:split $html "data-cx-frag=\""]]
                [where [$strings:contains $seg "[frag"]]
                [yield [$strings:replace [$first [$strings:split $seg "\""]] "&#10;" "\n"]]]]
  [= $missing [?for [in $m $minted]
                [where [not [$exists [?for [in $p $painted] [where [= $p $m]] [yield $p]]]]]
                [yield $m]]]
  [list [$count $minted] [$count $missing] $missing]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 34 0]
#]]
 ]

 [# THE GATE A USER FOUND BY CLICKING. `affects=` exists so a renderer derives
    its target from a semantic address rather than carrying a hand-written
    selector — but the PATH was still hand-written, and one that names nothing
    yields a target id for an element that does not exist. The swap then lands
    nowhere: the commit succeeds, the feed publishes, and the page silently does
    not update until a manual refresh, which looks like a caching problem.

    Every other address mistake here already refuses. This one did not. #]
 [case id=ux-105-a-dangling-affects-is-refused level=core
  [tags ux addressing refusal affects W5]
  [title 'an affects= naming a path nothing mints refuses instead of silently doing nothing']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/"}]
  [= $ok  [ux:region feature=orders path="/live"
            [ux:filter name=q affects="/live/open/rows"]
            [ux:table id=open [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=r1 [ux:cell "a"]]]]]]
  [= $bad [ux:region feature=orders path="/live"
            [ux:filter name=q affects="/live/typo/rows"]
            [ux:table id=open [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=r1 [ux:cell "a"]]]]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [list [?splice [$ux:dangling-affects $ok $ctx]] [?splice [$ux:dangling-affects $bad $ctx]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-dangling-affects element=ux:filter affects='/live/typo/rows']]
#]]
 ]

 [# A CX AUTHORING TRAP, pinned because it passed every two-row test. `$el/name` is
    a FIELD READ and atomizes on a single match, so [$first $li/a] returns the
    STRING for a one-anchor list item and the ELEMENT for a two-anchor one. Every
    fixture with two rows passes. `child-elements` goes through `/*` instead. #]
 [case id=ux-110-a-named-child-step-atomizes level=core
  [tags ux cx-trap child-axis]
  [title 'child-elements walks where a named step would hand back text']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $one [li [a href="/p" "Products"]]]
  [= $two [li [a href="/p" "Products"] [a href="/q" "More"]]]
  [list [$cx:canonical [$first $one/a]]
        [$cx:canonical [$ux:first-child $one "a"]]
        [$count [$ux:child-elements $two "a"]]
        [$ux:absent [$ux:first-child $one "zz"]]
        [$ux:absent [$ux:first-child $one "a"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'Products\n' '[a href=/p Products]\n' 2 true false]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-17 ruled that a reference-typed field projects against the referenced
    query. Nothing had built it, so the projection asked operators to type a
    catalogue id from memory. The options are the CALLER's half — only the
    caller can run the query behind the reference — and the control is the
    projection's. #]
 [case id=ux-036-a-reference-typed-field-projects-a-chooser level=core
  [tags ux form reference select P0-17 W6]
  [title 'a field whose type names another noun becomes a CHOOSER, not a text box']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $feature [$cx:parse "[feature name=shop [nouns [noun name=product [field name=sku type=text] [field name=name type=text]] [noun name=line [field name=sku type=product label='Product' doc='chosen, never typed'] [field name=qty type=int label='Qty']] [noun name=order [field name=customer type=text label='Customer'] [field name=lines type=line repeats=true add-label='Add a line']]] [verbs [verb name=place-order [summary 'Place an order.'] [intent [do :place-order [customer] [lines]]] [writes order]]]]"]]
  [= $opts {options: ([options for=product [option value=ESC-18 label="Escapement"] [option value=BAL-9 label="Balance wheel"]]),
            affects: "/f/entry", group-endpoint: true, group-affects: "/f/entry/place-order/lines"}]
  [= $f [$ux:feature-form $feature "place-order" $opts]]
  [$cx:canonical [$first $f//ux:select]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
"[ux:select name=lines.0.sku label=Product required='true' value=''\n  [ux:option value=ESC-18 Escapement]\n  [ux:option value=BAL-9 Balance wheel]\n]\n"
#]]
 ]

 [# The nested WRITE case. Every nested-case ruling in the spec is read-side; an
    order with two lines could be displayed and never entered. Instances are
    indexed in their control names because a form posts a flat key/value list
    and two instances under a bare name collapse to one. #]
 [case id=ux-037-a-repeating-field-projects-a-group level=core
  [tags ux form nested-write group W6]
  [title 'a repeating field projects a GROUP whose controls are indexed']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $feature [$cx:parse "[feature name=shop [nouns [noun name=product [field name=sku type=text] [field name=name type=text]] [noun name=line [field name=sku type=product label='Product' doc='chosen, never typed'] [field name=qty type=int label='Qty']] [noun name=order [field name=customer type=text label='Customer'] [field name=lines type=line repeats=true add-label='Add a line']]] [verbs [verb name=place-order [summary 'Place an order.'] [intent [do :place-order [customer] [lines]]] [writes order]]]]"]]
  [= $opts {options: ([options for=product [option value=ESC-18 label="Escapement"] [option value=BAL-9 label="Balance wheel"]]),
            affects: "/f/entry", group-endpoint: true, group-affects: "/f/entry/place-order/lines"}]
  [= $f [$ux:feature-form $feature "place-order" $opts]]
  [$cx:canonical [$first $f//ux:group]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
"[ux:group name=lines repeat='true' affects=/f/entry/place-order/lines add-label='Add a line'\n  [ux:card\n    [ux:select name=lines.0.sku label=Product required='true' value=''\n      [ux:option value=ESC-18 Escapement]\n      [ux:option value=BAL-9 Balance wheel]\n    ]\n    [ux:input name=lines.0.qty label=Qty kind=int value='']\n  ]\n]\n"
#]]
 ]

 [# A form that loses your input on an error is unusable, and it is the DEFAULT
    outcome of the obvious design: the command refuses, the handler renders the
    refusal, everything typed is gone. So the values are a render input, and
    the message rides the control the command layer named rather than a banner
    the operator has to hunt through. #]
 [case id=ux-038-a-refusal-keeps-what-was-typed level=core
  [tags ux form validation values W7]
  [title 'a re-projected form carries the operator’s input and the message on the field it belongs to']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $feature [$cx:parse "[feature name=shop [nouns [noun name=product [field name=sku type=text] [field name=name type=text]] [noun name=line [field name=sku type=product label='Product' doc='chosen, never typed'] [field name=qty type=int label='Qty']] [noun name=order [field name=customer type=text label='Customer'] [field name=lines type=line repeats=true add-label='Add a line']]] [verbs [verb name=place-order [summary 'Place an order.'] [intent [do :place-order [customer] [lines]]] [writes order]]]]"]]
  [= $opts {options: ([options for=product [option value=ESC-18 label="Escapement"] [option value=BAL-9 label="Balance wheel"]]),
            affects: "/f/entry", group-endpoint: true, group-affects: "/f/entry/place-order/lines"}]
  [= $vals ([val name=customer text=""], [val name="lines.0.sku" text="BAL-9"], [val name="lines.0.qty" text="2"])]
  [= $errs ([field-error name=customer message="who is this order for?"])]
  [= $f [$ux:feature-form $feature "place-order" [$cx:merge $opts {values: $vals, errors: $errs}]]]
  [$cx:canonical [$first $f//ux:input]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
"[ux:input name=customer label=Customer kind=text value='' error='who is this order for?']\n"
#]]
 ]

 [# Drop the first of two lines and the survivor is `lines.1`. Re-render "two
    instances" as 0 and 1 and the survivor's typed values are looked up under
    `lines.0`, find nothing, and come back blank — the same data loss the
    values were threaded through the projection to prevent. Indices are the
    instance's identity on the wire, so indices are what is preserved. #]
 [case id=ux-039-instances-are-preserved-by-index-not-by-count level=core
  [tags ux form group indices W7]
  [title 'removing a line re-renders the SURVIVOR, not a renumbered blank']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $feature [$cx:parse "[feature name=shop [nouns [noun name=product [field name=sku type=text] [field name=name type=text]] [noun name=line [field name=sku type=product label='Product' doc='chosen, never typed'] [field name=qty type=int label='Qty']] [noun name=order [field name=customer type=text label='Customer'] [field name=lines type=line repeats=true add-label='Add a line']]] [verbs [verb name=place-order [summary 'Place an order.'] [intent [do :place-order [customer] [lines]]] [writes order]]]]"]]
  [= $opts {options: ([options for=product [option value=ESC-18 label="Escapement"] [option value=BAL-9 label="Balance wheel"]]),
            affects: "/f/entry", group-endpoint: true, group-affects: "/f/entry/place-order/lines"}]
  [= $vals ([val name="lines.1.sku" text="BAL-9"], [val name="lines.1.qty" text="3"])]
  [= $f [$ux:feature-form $feature "place-order" [$cx:merge $opts {values: $vals}]]]
  [= $g [$first $f//ux:group]]
  [list [$count $g] [$string [$first [?for [in $s $g//ux:select] [yield $s@name]]]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 1 'lines.1.sku']
#]]
 ]

 [# Found by a bug. [$concat] was handed a node-set; the builtin's refusal came
    back as an [err] ELEMENT, the renderer treated it as an ordinary child, and
    it serialized into the page as content with a 200. Every gate passed,
    because none was looking for a refusal that had stopped being a refusal and
    become a node. Refusals travel on the [err] CHANNEL; one that has been
    spliced into a document has already lost what made it a refusal. #]
 [case id=ux-042-an-err-in-the-tree-refuses-the-render level=core
  [tags ux refusal err-in-tree W7 §5.4]
  [title 'an [err] anywhere in a tree destined for a surface refuses the whole render']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $t [ux:card [ux:text "fine"] [err code=cx-err:CXER0100 message="concat: bad argument"]]]
  [$cx:canonical [ux-refusals [?splice [$ux:err-refusals $t]]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
"[ux-refusals\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-err-in-tree element=cx-err:CXER0100 affects='concat: bad argument']\n]\n"
#]]
 ]

 [# `label-of-name` turns `promised-at` into "Promised at", which is right when
    the document says nothing. When the document DOES say — `label='Product'`
    on a field named `sku` — the derivation is a worse answer than the one the
    author already gave. #]
 [case id=ux-043-a-grammar-supplied-label-wins-over-the-derived-one level=core
  [tags ux form labels grammar W7]
  [title 'a field’s label comes from the grammar when the grammar states one']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $feature [$cx:parse "[feature name=shop [nouns [noun name=product [field name=sku type=text] [field name=name type=text]] [noun name=line [field name=sku type=product label='Product' doc='chosen, never typed'] [field name=qty type=int label='Qty']] [noun name=order [field name=customer type=text label='Customer'] [field name=lines type=line repeats=true add-label='Add a line']]] [verbs [verb name=place-order [summary 'Place an order.'] [intent [do :place-order [customer] [lines]]] [writes order]]]]"]]
  [= $opts {options: ([options for=product [option value=ESC-18 label="Escapement"] [option value=BAL-9 label="Balance wheel"]]),
            affects: "/f/entry", group-endpoint: true, group-affects: "/f/entry/place-order/lines"}]
  [= $f [$ux:feature-form $feature "place-order" $opts]]
  [?for [in $c $f//*] [where [$exists $c@label]] [yield [$string $c@label]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'Customer'
'Product'
'Qty'
'Place order'
#]]
 ]

 [# An undescribed depiction is a refusal, not a rendering with an empty
    attribute; decorative art says alt='' explicitly, so 'described as
    decorative' and 'nobody wrote it' are distinguishable states (P0-95). #]
 [case id=ux-060-media level=core
  [tags ux member media refusal content P0-54 P0-95 W10]
  [title 'a depiction requires src and alt; alt=’’ is a stated decoration; kind/tone are not content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:media alt="a pan"]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:media src="/a.svg"]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:media src="/a.svg" alt=""]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:media src="/a.svg" alt="a pan" kind=illustration tone=neutral]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-media-no-src element=ux:media] [ux-refusal code=ux-media-no-alt element=ux:media src='/a.svg'] false [c:media src='/a.svg' alt='a pan']]
#]]
 ]

 [# A table's members are rows of one shape compared field by field; a grid's
    are whole things compared as wholes — a difference in what the reader is
    doing, not in appearance (P0-55). A column count is never a member
    attribute, because the vocabulary has no viewport. #]
 [case id=ux-061-grid level=core
  [tags ux member grid content P0-55 W10]
  [title 'a grid is peers compared as wholes; density is a hint, never content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:grid density=comfortable [ux:item "a"] [ux:item "b"]]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:grid density=compact [ux:item "a"] [ux:item "b"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list false [c:grid [c:item 'a'] [c:item 'b']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# A parameter with no value posts an empty string and the command layer then
    blames the customer for the projection's bug (P0-56). Refusing at
    projection puts the error where the mistake is. #]
 [case id=ux-062-param level=core
  [tags ux member param refusal content P0-56 W10]
  [title 'a parameter needs a name and a value; both are content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:param value=x]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:param name=sku]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:param name=sku value=FK-0119]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:param] [ux-refusal code=ux-param-no-value element=ux:param param=sku] [c:param name=sku value=FK-0119]]
#]]
 ]

 [# The name scopes the sequence's address; current is content because 'where
    you are' is the member's whole meaning. #]
 [case id=ux-063-steps level=core
  [tags ux member steps refusal content P0-57 W10]
  [title 'a step sequence is named, and says which step is current']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:steps current=address]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:steps name=checkout current=shipping
                        [ux:step name=address state=done href="/checkout/address" label="Address"]
                        [ux:step name=shipping state=current label="Shipping"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:steps] [c:steps name=checkout current=shipping [c:step name=address state=done href='/checkout/address' 'Address'] [c:step name=shipping state=current href='' 'Shipping']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# 'You cannot go back and change the address you already typed' is the most
    commonly lost property of a checkout, and its loss is silent — the one
    place a UX pattern is enforced by the vocabulary rather than by review. #]
 [case id=ux-064-step level=core
  [tags ux member step refusal content P0-57 P0-89 W10]
  [title 'a done step MUST carry an href; a state outside the trichotomy refuses']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:step name=a state=done label=A]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:step name=a state=someday label=A]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:step name=a state=done href="/checkout/a" label=A]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:step name=a state=ahead label="Address"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-step-not-editable element=ux:step step=a] [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-step-state element=ux:step state=someday] false [c:step name=a state=ahead href='' 'Address']]
#]]
 ]

 [# Act mode: each change is a real intent (P0-82); at a bound the affordance is
    disabled, never absent (P0-58). FIELD mode (W23, audit2 letter (i)): no verb,
    the value rides the enclosing form's post and the shell steps it locally —
    the content states verb empty, so no face can silently disagree about which
    mode it renders. #]
 [case id=ux-065-quantity level=core
  [tags ux member quantity refusal content P0-58 P0-102 W10 W23]
  [title 'a bounded quantity needs a name; with a verb every step is an intent, without one it is the enclosing form’s field']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:quantity verb=set-quantity]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:quantity name=qty]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:quantity name=qty label=Quantity verb=set-quantity value=2 min=1 max=9
                        [ux:param name=sku value=FK-0119]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:quantity name=qty label=Quantity value=2 min=1 max=9]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:quantity] false [c:quantity name=qty label=Quantity verb=set-quantity value='2' min='1' max='9' [c:param name=sku value=FK-0119]] [c:quantity name=qty label=Quantity verb='' value='2' min='1' max='9']]
#]]
 ]

 [# No payload rule of its own — the rules live on the crumbs — but the member
    must survive the normal form or a face could drop the trail silently. #]
 [case id=ux-066-breadcrumb level=core
  [tags ux member breadcrumb content P0-59 W10]
  [title 'where you are, as structure; the crumbs are its content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:breadcrumb [ux:crumb href="/" label=ORIEL] [ux:crumb current=true label=Pans]]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:breadcrumb [ux:crumb href="/" label=ORIEL] [ux:crumb current=true label=Pans]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list false [c:breadcrumb [c:crumb href='/' current='false' 'ORIEL'] [c:crumb href='' current='true' 'Pans']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# A link to where you already are is a lie about what activating it does, and
    in a terminal it is a focus stop that does nothing. #]
 [case id=ux-067-crumb level=core
  [tags ux member crumb refusal content P0-59 W10]
  [title 'the current crumb carries no href; every crumb carries a label']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:crumb current=true href="/here" label=Here]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:crumb href="/x"]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:crumb href="/c/kitchen" label=Kitchen]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-crumb-current-href element=ux:crumb] [ux-refusal code=ux-crumb-no-label element=ux:crumb] [c:crumb href='/c/kitchen' current='false' 'Kitchen']]
#]]
 ]

 [# The same request twice may return overlapping pages and omit rows entirely,
    and no client can detect it — so the collection has to SAY it is ordered;
    silence is refused rather than assumed (P0-60). #]
 [case id=ux-068-pager level=core
  [tags ux member pager refusal content P0-60 W10]
  [title 'paging an unordered collection is a lottery, and is refused']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:pager page=1 pages=3 total=61]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:pager ordered=true pages=3]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:pager ordered=true page=2 pages=3 total=61
                        prev-href="/c/x?p=1" next-href="/c/x?p=3"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-pager-unordered element=ux:pager] [ux-refusal code=ux-pager-no-page element=ux:pager] [c:pager page='2' pages='3' total='61' prev-href='/c/x?p=1' next-href='/c/x?p=3']]
#]]
 ]

 [# Same rule as every navigation member: an affordance that leads nowhere is
    inert-but-rendered, the failure class that survives every server-side gate. #]
 [case id=ux-069-page-link level=core
  [tags ux member page-link refusal content P0-60 W10]
  [title 'a page link is a place; without an href it is a trap']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:page-link n=2]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:page-link n=2 href="/c/x?p=2" current=true]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-link-no-href element=ux:page-link] [c:page-link n='2' href='/c/x?p=2' current='true']]
#]]
 ]

 [# The counts are the whole difference from ux:filter — a filter tells you
    nothing until you use it (P0-61). #]
 [case id=ux-070-facet level=core
  [tags ux member facet refusal content P0-61 W10]
  [title 'a facet is named; its label and values are its content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:facet label=Brand]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:facet name=brand label=Brand
                        [ux:facet-value value=farrow label=Farrow count=12 href="/c/x?brand=farrow"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:facet] [c:facet name=brand label=Brand [c:facet-value value=farrow count='12' href='/c/x?brand=farrow' selected='false' 'Farrow']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# A dead-end affordance leads to an empty page; it is admitted only when
    SELECTED, because then it is the only way back out of the empty page you
    are already looking at. #]
 [case id=ux-071-facet-value level=core
  [tags ux member facet-value refusal content P0-61 W10]
  [title 'a zero-count value is a trap with a label on it — unless it is the way back out']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:facet-value value=x label=X count=0 href="/c?f=x"]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:facet-value value=x label=X count=0 selected=true href="/c"]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:facet-value value=x label=X count=3]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:facet-value value=farrow label=Farrow count=12 selected=true href="/c/x?brand=farrow"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-facet-dead-end element=ux:facet-value value=x] false [ux-refusal code=ux-link-no-href element=ux:facet-value] [c:facet-value value=farrow count='12' href='/c/x?brand=farrow' selected='true' 'Farrow']]
#]]
 ]

 [# W7 deferred it web-first and the parity ruling brought it back in both
    faces at once (P0-62). #]
 [case id=ux-072-sort level=core
  [tags ux member sort refusal content P0-62 W10]
  [title 'a sort is named; the chosen order is a URL, not a client state']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:sort label="Sort by"]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:sort name=sort label="Sort by"
                        [ux:sort-option value=rating label="Customer rating" selected=true href="/c/x?sort=rating"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:sort] [c:sort name=sort label='Sort by' [c:sort-option value=rating href='/c/x?sort=rating' selected='true' 'Customer rating']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Choosing an order is a navigation — the ordered collection is a different
    meaningful state, and a meaningful state is a URL (P0-78). #]
 [case id=ux-073-sort-option level=core
  [tags ux member sort-option refusal content P0-62 W10]
  [title 'a sort option is a place; value, selection and label are content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:sort-option value=rating label=Rating]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:sort-option value=rating label=Rating selected=false href="/c/x?sort=rating"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-link-no-href element=ux:sort-option] [c:sort-option value=rating href='/c/x?sort=rating' selected='false' 'Rating']]
#]]
 ]

 [# Its open state is a URL and its dismissal is a NAVIGATION — admitted over
    its own objection (DP2 letter c), and the dismiss rule is why the
    objection lost. #]
 [case id=ux-074-aside level=core
  [tags ux member aside refusal content P0-63 W10]
  [title 'an aside is labelled; open without a way out is a trap']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:aside open=true dismiss-href="/x"]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:aside label=Basket open=true]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:aside label=Basket open=false]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:aside label="Your basket" open=true dismiss-href="/basket?b=closed"
                        [ux:text "Empty."]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-aside-no-label element=ux:aside] [ux-refusal code=ux-aside-no-dismiss element=ux:aside label=Basket] false [c:aside label='Your basket' open='true' dismiss-href='/basket?b=closed' [c:text 'Empty.']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Before this member a screen-reader user got silence on every swap; and a
    live region inserted at the same moment as its first message is not
    announced, so an EMPTY status is the correct first paint (P0-93). #]
 [case id=ux-075-status level=core
  [tags ux member status refusal content P0-64 P0-93 W10]
  [title 'an announcement carries a tone from the closed set; empty is a legal first paint']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:status tone=sparkly "x"]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:status tone=ok "Added to basket"]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:status tone=ok "Added to basket"]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:status tone=info ""]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-tone element=ux:status tone=sparkly] false [c:status tone=ok 'Added to basket'] [c:status tone=info '']]
#]]
 ]

 [# 'These blocks are peers, and the group has a name' is a statement about the
    content's structure — the argument that admitted ux:group for writes.
    Peers of peers is a hierarchy, and a hierarchy is what regions and cards
    already are, so nesting refuses (ux-panel-nested). The label defaults
    from the name and the grouping is CONTENT: a face that dropped it would
    flatten a case file's parties and timeline into one undifferentiated run. #]
 [case id=ux-076-panel level=core
  [tags ux member panel refusal content P0-101 DP4]
  [title 'a panel is a named group of peers; unnamed refuses, a panel in a panel refuses']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[list [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:panel [ux:text "x"]]]]
      [?splice [$ux:refusals-of [ux:panel name=a [ux:card [ux:panel name=b [ux:text "x"]]]]]]
      [$exists [$ux:refusals-of [ux:panel name=narrow [ux:facet name=brand label=Brand
                                   [ux:facet-value value=v label=V count=2 href="/c?b=v"]]]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:panel name=narrow [ux:text "x"]]]
      [$ux:content-of [ux:panel name=parties label="The parties" [ux:field label=Claimant "Ada"]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-input-no-name element=ux:panel] [ux-refusal code=ux-panel-nested element=ux:panel panel=a] false [c:panel name=narrow label=Narrow [c:text 'x']] [c:panel name=parties label='The parties' [c:field label=Claimant 'Ada']]]
#]]
 ]

 [# ── §13: the layout-command engine (W26, the studio; P0-105…P0-108) ─────────
    The five commands applied to an arrangement-as-data layout document. Pure:
    the journal entry, the actor stamp and the SSE publish are the serving
    boundary's, which is what makes every refusal class a fixture. #]

 [# P0-26/P0-105: a move rearranges ONLY what it addresses — the unaddressed
    sibling keeps its place, its attributes and (absent) surface hints. #]
 [case id=ux-105-layout-move-untouched level=core
  [tags ux layout studio move P0-26 P0-105 W26]
  [title 'a move touches the addressed element and nothing else']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $comps ([component name=hero], [component name=departments], [component name=promo], [component name=group container=true])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero] [placed id=promo component=promo] [placed id=depts component=departments]]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:move id=depts parent="" before=hero] $comps]]
  [list [$cx:canonical $l1] [$ux:layout-hint $l1 promo tone]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '[ux:layout\n  [placed id=depts component=departments]\n  [placed id=hero component=hero]\n  [placed id=promo component=promo]\n]\n' '']
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-28: an address miss is a REFUSAL, not a no-op — silence would hide a
    lost update. #]
 [case id=ux-106-layout-address-miss level=core
  [tags ux layout studio refusal P0-28 W26]
  [title 'a command addressing an id that does not exist refuses']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $comps ([component name=hero])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero]]]
  [= $r [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:move id=ghost parent="" before=""] $comps]]
  [$cx:canonical ($r)]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-address-miss id=ghost]\n]\n)\n'
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-105/P0-16: place inserts under an author-assigned id, uniqueness checked
    at apply (collision refuses); placeable is a cascade-resolvable component
    name, never raw markup (unknown component refuses). #]
 [case id=ux-107-layout-place-collision-and-unknown-component level=core
  [tags ux layout studio place refusal P0-16 P0-105 W26]
  [title 'place inserts; a colliding id refuses; an unresolvable component refuses']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $comps ([component name=hero], [component name=promo])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero]]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:place id=promo component=promo parent="" before=hero] $comps]]
  [= $r1 [$ux:layout-apply $l1 [ux:place id=promo component=promo parent="" before=""] $comps]]
  [= $r2 [$ux:layout-apply $l1 [ux:place id=x component=nonesuch parent="" before=""] $comps]]
  [list [$cx:canonical $l1] [$cx:canonical ($r1)] [$cx:canonical ($r2)]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '[ux:layout\n  [placed id=promo component=promo]\n  [placed id=hero component=hero]\n]\n' '([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-id-collision id=promo]\n]\n)\n' '([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-unknown-component component=nonesuch]\n]\n)\n']
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-106: remove takes the addressed element's OWN subtree — children are
    its content; the unaddressed sibling survives. The remove-inverse is the
    P0-27 batch that reconstructs the subtree in place: the place, its
    surface hint, its child's place — in that order. #]
 [case id=ux-108-layout-remove-takes-its-subtree level=core
  [tags ux layout studio remove inverse P0-106 P0-108 W26]
  [title 'remove drops the addressed subtree, spares siblings, and its inverse reconstructs it']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $comps ([component name=hero], [component name=promo], [component name=group container=true])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout
           [placed id=rail component=group [hint name=density value=compact]
             [placed id=promo component=promo]]
           [placed id=hero component=hero]]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:remove id=rail] $comps]]
  [list [$cx:canonical $l1] [$cx:canonical [x [?splice [$ux:layout-inverse $l0 [ux:remove id=rail]]]]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '[ux:layout\n  [placed id=hero component=hero]\n]\n' "[x\n  [ux:place id=rail component=group parent='' before=hero]\n  [ux:set-hint id=rail hint=density value=compact]\n  [ux:place id=promo component=promo parent=rail before='']\n]\n"]
#]]
 ]

 [# THE LENSED-EDITOR CASE (P0-26/P0-29), the fixture §7 names highest-value:
    the full document holds three placed elements; the editor's lens withheld
    `featured`; the move the editor emits — computed from what they saw — is
    applied against the FULL document server-side, and the withheld element
    survives untouched, still in sequence. #]
 [case id=ux-109-layout-lensed-editor level=core
  [tags ux layout studio lens P0-26 P0-29 W26]
  [title 'an editor under a reduced projection edits what it sees; withheld elements survive']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $comps ([component name=hero], [component name=featured], [component name=departments])]
  [= $full [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero] [placed id=featured component=featured] [placed id=depts component=departments]]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $full [ux:move id=depts parent="" before=hero] $comps]]
  [list [$cx:canonical $l1]
        [$ux:found [$ux:layout-find $l1 featured]]
        [$ux:layout-successor $l1 hero]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '[ux:layout\n  [placed id=depts component=departments]\n  [placed id=hero component=hero]\n  [placed id=featured component=featured]\n]\n' true 'featured']
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-105/P0-29 validity, judged against the full document server-side: wrap
    mints a container standing where the target stood; a cycle refuses; a
    non-container wrapper component refuses; a non-container parent refuses. #]
 [case id=ux-110-layout-wrap-and-validity level=core
  [tags ux layout studio wrap refusal cycle P0-29 P0-105 W26]
  [title 'wrap wraps in place; cycles and non-containers refuse']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $comps ([component name=hero], [component name=promo], [component name=group container=true])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero] [placed id=promo component=promo]]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:wrap id=hero wrapper=g1 component=group] $comps]]
  [= $r1 [$ux:layout-apply $l1 [ux:move id=g1 parent=g1 before=""] $comps]]
  [= $r2 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:wrap id=hero wrapper=g2 component=promo] $comps]]
  [= $r3 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:move id=hero parent=promo before=""] $comps]]
  [list [$cx:canonical $l1] [$cx:canonical ($r1)] [$cx:canonical ($r2)] [$cx:canonical ($r3)]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '[ux:layout\n  [placed id=g1 component=group\n    [placed id=hero component=hero]\n  ]\n  [placed id=promo component=promo]\n]\n' '([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-cycle id=g1 parent=g1]\n]\n)\n' '([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-not-container component=promo]\n]\n)\n' '([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-not-container id=promo]\n]\n)\n']
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-108, THE INVERSE-PAIR TABLE, round-tripped whole: for each of the five
    commands, apply then apply-the-inverse restores the original document
    byte-identically. The wrap inverse is the ruled P0-27 batch (move the
    child back, THEN remove the empty wrapper); the remove inverse is the
    reconstructing place batch. #]
 [case id=ux-111-layout-inverse-pairs level=core
  [tags ux layout studio inverse undo P0-27 P0-108 W26]
  [title 'every command round-trips through its inverse to byte-identical bytes']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $comps ([component name=hero], [component name=featured], [component name=promo], [component name=group container=true])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero] [placed id=featured component=featured]]]
  [= $rt [?fn ($cmd)
           [?let [= $inv [$ux:layout-inverse $l0 $cmd]]
             [= [$cx:canonical [$ux:layout-apply-batch [$ux:layout-apply $l0 $cmd $comps] $inv $comps]]
                [$cx:canonical $l0]]]]]
  [list [$rt [ux:move id=featured parent="" before=hero]]
        [$rt [ux:set-hint id=hero hint=tone value=info]]
        [$rt [ux:place id=promo component=promo parent="" before=""]]
        [$rt [ux:wrap id=hero wrapper=g1 component=group]]
        [$rt [ux:remove id=hero]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list true true true true true]
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-107: a set-hint lands at the SURFACE level (the placed element's own
    hint children); value="" clears it and the inherited resolution applies
    again — the document returns to its pre-hint bytes. #]
 [case id=ux-112-layout-set-hint-surface-level level=core
  [tags ux layout studio set-hint P0-107 W26]
  [title 'set-hint writes and clears at the surface level']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $comps ([component name=departments])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=depts component=departments]]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:set-hint id=depts hint=density value=compact] $comps]]
  [= $l2 [$ux:layout-apply $l1 [ux:set-hint id=depts hint=density value=""] $comps]]
  [list [$ux:layout-hint $l0 depts density] [$ux:layout-hint $l1 depts density]
        [$cx:canonical $l2]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '' 'compact' '[ux:layout\n  [placed id=depts component=departments]\n]\n']
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-27: a batch is atomic — one journal entry, all-or-nothing. A refusal of
    any member refuses the batch whole; the first member's edit is not kept. #]
 [case id=ux-113-layout-batch-atomic level=core
  [tags ux layout studio batch refusal P0-27 W26]
  [title 'a batch with a refusing member refuses whole']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [= $comps ([component name=hero])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $r [$ux:layout-apply-batch $l0 ([ux:set-hint id=hero hint=tone value=info], [ux:remove id=ghost]) $comps]]
  [$cx:canonical ($r)]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-address-miss id=ghost]\n]\n)\n'
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-111 (W27, the designer's studio): set-param joins the closed command
    set — content params on placed elements, declared by the registry (an
    undeclared param refuses: the registry IS the closed editing surface,
    P0-113), value="" returning to the default, the inverse restoring the
    prior, and the remove-inverse reconstructing params with the subtree. #]
 [case id=ux-114-set-param-content-surface level=core
  [tags ux layout studio set-param P0-111 P0-113 W27]
  [title 'a content param writes, refuses undeclared names, and round-trips its inverse']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $comps ([component name=hero [param name=heading default="Everyday things." carrier=ux-heading]],
             [component name=promo [param name=text default="A note." carrier=ux-text]])]
  [= $l0 [ux:layout [placed id=hero component=hero] [placed id=promo component=promo]]]
  [= $c1 [ux:set-param id=hero param=heading value="Made properly."]]
  [= $l1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 $c1 $comps]]
  [= $r1 [$ux:layout-apply $l0 [ux:set-param id=hero param=nonesuch value=x] $comps]]
  [= $inv [$ux:layout-inverse $l0 $c1]]
  [= $l2 [$ux:layout-apply-batch $l1 $inv $comps]]
  [list [$cx:canonical $l1]
        [$ux:layout-param $l1 hero heading]
        [$cx:canonical ($r1)]
        [= [$cx:canonical $l2] [$cx:canonical $l0]]
        [$cx:canonical [x [?splice [$ux:layout-inverse $l1 [ux:remove id=hero]]]]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list "[ux:layout\n  [placed id=hero component=hero\n    [param name=heading value='Made properly.']\n  ]\n  [placed id=promo component=promo]\n]\n" 'Made properly.' '([err code=ux-refused\n  [ux-refusal code=ux-layout-unknown-param id=hero param=nonesuch]\n]\n)\n' true "[x\n  [ux:place id=hero component=hero parent='' before=promo]\n  [ux:set-param id=hero param=heading value='Made properly.']\n]\n"]
#]]
 ]
]
